162
00:18:30,003 --> 00:18:37,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the restraint is the necessary precondition for arising of wisdom, for arising of the understanding of the nature of things.

163
00:18:42,390 --> 00:18:47,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But with like, if you, if you restrain yourself, you still have the, that pull.

164
00:18:47,932 --> 00:18:49,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, the pain with the senses is still there.

165
00:18:50,053 --> 00:18:50,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

166
00:18:50,293 --> 00:18:54,075
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not going further and getting more, you know, feverish.

167
00:18:54,095 --> 00:18:58,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the pain, the pain will be there for as long as it doesn't sink deep enough.

168
00:18:59,237 --> 00:19:02,358
[SPEAKER_00]: If the pain does not belong to restraint, it belongs to the senses.

169
00:19:02,859 --> 00:19:05,960
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that very reason, the restraint is actually quite pleasant.

170
00:19:07,661 --> 00:19:10,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Fire will always be hot, even a hundred miles away from you.

171
00:19:11,955 --> 00:19:18,197
[SPEAKER_00]: But now that you're not pulled towards it, and you know there is no danger of you falling into that burning pit, here it is.

172
00:19:18,477 --> 00:19:18,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

173
00:19:18,837 --> 00:19:19,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of your restraint.

174
00:19:20,097 --> 00:19:21,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't go anywhere near it.

175
00:19:24,198 --> 00:19:26,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the pleasure of renunciation.

176
00:19:26,979 --> 00:19:29,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Always trumps the pleasure of sensuality.

177
00:19:32,421 --> 00:19:34,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the pleasure of sensuality is always very valuable.

178
00:19:35,582 --> 00:19:36,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

179
00:19:36,682 --> 00:19:39,023
[SPEAKER_00]: But your mind will go everywhere else.

180
00:19:40,790 --> 00:19:43,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Away from that central pleasure.

181
00:19:43,971 --> 00:19:49,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Like that man who was being dragged by the two stronger men to the pit, he would want to be everywhere else but that pit.

182
00:19:50,033 --> 00:20:04,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you recognize that the whole pain, the whole kind of drama and proliferation and everything and mess that comes with it, it's all on account of the central pleasures.

183
00:20:07,086 --> 00:20:12,127
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you have to do is say no to it and you're literally free from that entire domain of misfortune.

184
00:20:13,547 --> 00:20:30,991
[SPEAKER_00]: And then there was another sutra where the Buddha did say all the... fathers fight with sons, sons fight with mothers, brothers with sisters, brothers with brothers, kings with kings, nations with nations, all on account of sensual pleasures, all on account of sensuality.

185
00:20:31,731 --> 00:20:35,872
[SPEAKER_00]: All of the wars that are being waged, that will be waged, it's all on account of sensuality.

186
00:20:36,590 --> 00:20:37,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing works.

187
00:20:40,472 --> 00:20:53,360
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... it's... all of that might not mean anything if a person on that individual, private, personal level, still don't recognize the pain of sensuality belonging to the actual sensuality.

188
00:20:54,521 --> 00:20:58,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pain of saying no to sensuality, belonging to the actual sensuality.

189
00:20:59,084 --> 00:21:00,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to you saying no.

190
00:21:02,786 --> 00:21:04,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no, merely reveals it.

191
00:21:08,061 --> 00:21:14,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose, Magandya, there was a leper with sores and blisters on his limbs, being devoured by worms.

192
00:21:16,363 --> 00:21:17,303
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just what I said.

193
00:21:17,323 --> 00:21:20,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, the more you cover it up, the more you'll need to cover it up.

194
00:21:46,654 --> 00:21:49,716
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you'll assume that covering up is sorting it out.

195
00:21:50,657 --> 00:21:57,803
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will get some satisfaction in pursuing desire, because, basically, it makes you forget about the pain caused by the very desire.

196
00:21:58,464 --> 00:22:04,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it's become a more delusional... Because that's all it can do, make you forget about the pain.

197
00:22:05,330 --> 00:22:09,173
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you can never satisfy a desire.

198
00:22:09,573 --> 00:22:10,995
[SPEAKER_00]: In its nature, it's insatiable.

199
00:22:11,944 --> 00:22:18,026
[SPEAKER_00]: For the very reason that all you can do is forget about the pain, the pang, the dart that it causes to you.

200
00:22:19,746 --> 00:22:23,468
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people like to elaborate on their desires, not just go and satisfy them.

201
00:22:25,048 --> 00:22:30,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it provides a more elaborate, forgetful cover-up of the of the actual pain.

202
00:22:33,170 --> 00:22:38,192
[SPEAKER_00]: But only so for that dart to arise again, to hit you again.

203
00:22:41,120 --> 00:22:48,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when your senses do break apart, when you can't use any of those domains for this game of cover-up, then you're just left with the pain.

204
00:22:49,734 --> 00:22:54,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why old age can be very hard for those who depend on their senses more than others.

205
00:22:59,619 --> 00:23:00,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma continues.

206
00:23:00,940 --> 00:23:11,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So too, Magandhiya, beings who are not thief and lust for sensual pleasures, who are devoured by craving for sensual pleasures, who burn with the fever for sensual pleasures, still indulge in sensual pleasures.

207
00:23:12,527 --> 00:23:21,593
[SPEAKER_01]: The more such beings indulge in sensual pleasures, the more their craving for sensual pleasures increases, and the more they are burned with their fever for sensual pleasures.

208
00:23:21,733 --> 00:23:27,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they find a certain measure of satisfaction and enjoyment, independent from the fire called sensual pleasure.

209
00:23:32,340 --> 00:23:33,441
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, Magandhiya?

210
00:23:33,761 --> 00:23:40,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you ever seen or heard of a king or a king's minister enjoying himself, provided and endowed with the five codes of sensual pleasure?

211
00:23:41,067 --> 00:23:51,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Who, without abandoning, craving for sensual pleasures, without removing fever for sensual pleasures, was able to abide free from thirst, with a mind inwardly at peace?

212
00:23:51,976 --> 00:23:56,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Or who is able, or who will be able to abide, to so abide?

213
00:23:57,620 --> 00:23:58,661
[SPEAKER_01]: No, Master Gautam.

214
00:23:59,636 --> 00:24:19,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Good, Vagandhīr, I too have never seen or heard of a king or a king's minister enjoying himself without abandoning craving for sensual pleasures, without removing fever for sensual pleasures, who is able to abide free from thirst with the mind inwardly at peace, or who is, who is able, or who will be able to so abide.

215
00:24:20,943 --> 00:24:42,384
[SPEAKER_01]: On the contrary, Magandhiya, those recluses of Rāmas who abided, or abide, or will abide, free from thirst, with the mind inwardly at peace, all do so after having understood, as they actually are, the origin, the disappearance, the gratification, the danger, and the escape in the case of sensual pleasures.

216
00:24:43,005 --> 00:24:54,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is after abandoning, craving for sensual pleasures, and removing people from sensual pleasures, that they are guided, or abide, or will abide, freedom first, with a mind inwardly of peace.

217
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:02,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, at that point, the Blessed One uttered these exclamations.

218
00:25:03,445 --> 00:25:05,447
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest of all games is health.

219
00:25:06,297 --> 00:25:07,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna is the greatest bliss.

220
00:25:08,959 --> 00:25:13,743
[SPEAKER_01]: The eightfold path is the best of paths, for it leads safely to the deathless.

221
00:25:15,184 --> 00:25:21,328
[SPEAKER_01]: When this was said, the wanderer Magandiya said to the Blessed One, It is wonderful, Master Gautama.

222
00:25:21,408 --> 00:25:25,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It is marvelous how well that has been expressed by Master Gautama.

223
00:25:25,751 --> 00:25:27,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest of all gains is health.

224
00:25:28,413 --> 00:25:30,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna is the greatest bliss.

225
00:25:31,595 --> 00:25:38,097
[SPEAKER_01]: We, too, have heard earlier Wanderers, who were teachers of teachers, saying this, and in degrees with Master Gautama.

226
00:25:39,778 --> 00:25:47,341
[SPEAKER_01]: But, Magandiya, when you heard earlier Wanderers, who were teachers and teachers of teachers, saying this, what is it?

227
00:25:47,841 --> 00:25:49,081
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that health?

228
00:25:49,402 --> 00:25:50,502
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that nibbāna?

229
00:25:51,876 --> 00:25:58,718
[SPEAKER_01]: When this was said, the wanderer Magandya rubbed his own limb with his hands and said, This is that health, Master Gautama.

230
00:25:59,098 --> 00:26:00,179
[SPEAKER_01]: This is that nibbāna.

231
00:26:00,559 --> 00:26:03,540
[SPEAKER_01]: For I am now healthy and happy, and nothing afflicts me.

232
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:06,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, bless it.

233
00:26:07,301 --> 00:26:09,201
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes on with the simile.

234
00:26:10,081 --> 00:26:21,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Magandya, suppose there was a man born blind who could not see dark and light forms, who could not see blue, yellow, red or pink forms, who could not see what was even and uneven.

235
00:26:22,656 --> 00:26:36,188
[SPEAKER_01]: You might hear a man with good eyesight say, Then he would go in search of a white cloth.

236
00:26:37,109 --> 00:26:39,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Then a man would cheat him with a dirty soiled garment.

237
00:26:39,991 --> 00:26:42,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, Good man, here is a white cloth for you.

238
00:26:43,234 --> 00:26:44,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Beautiful, spotless and clean.

239
00:26:45,396 --> 00:26:46,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he would accept it and put it on.

240
00:26:47,518 --> 00:26:50,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, being satisfied with it, he would utter words of satisfaction.

241
00:26:50,962 --> 00:26:55,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, good indeed, sirs, is a white cloth, beautiful, spotless and clean.

242
00:26:56,274 --> 00:27:07,063
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, Magandib, when that man, born blind, accepted that dirty soil of the garment, put it on, and, being satisfied with it, uttered words of satisfaction thus?

243
00:27:07,843 --> 00:27:11,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Good indeed, sirs, is a white cloth, beautiful, spotless, and clean.

244
00:27:12,527 --> 00:27:16,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he do so knowing and seeing, or out of faith in a man with good eyesight?

245
00:27:17,891 --> 00:27:23,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable sir, he would have done so unknowing and unseen, out of faith in a man with good eyesight.

246
00:27:25,167 --> 00:27:30,094
[SPEAKER_01]: So too, Magandha, the wanderers of other sects, are blind in visions.

247
00:27:30,875 --> 00:27:31,916
[SPEAKER_01]: They do not know health.

248
00:27:32,177 --> 00:27:33,398
[SPEAKER_01]: They do not see Nibbāna.

249
00:27:33,879 --> 00:27:35,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet they utter this stanza thus.

250
00:27:36,162 --> 00:27:38,005
[SPEAKER_01]: The greatest of all games is health.

251
00:27:38,566 --> 00:27:40,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna is the greatest bliss.

252
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:47,026
[SPEAKER_01]: This stanza was uttered by the earlier accomplished ones, fully enlightened ones.

253
00:27:47,246 --> 00:27:49,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the greatest of all gains is health.

254
00:27:50,289 --> 00:27:51,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna is the greatest bliss.

255
00:27:52,451 --> 00:27:57,336
[SPEAKER_01]: The eightfold path is the best of paths, for it leads to Saipu, to the Vyāsas.

256
00:27:59,078 --> 00:28:02,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it has gradually become current among ordinary people.

257
00:28:03,470 --> 00:28:17,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And although this body, Magandhya, is a disease, a tumor, a dart, a calamity, and an affliction, referring to this body, you say, this is that health, Master Gautama, this is that nibbāna.

258
00:28:18,026 --> 00:28:24,353
[SPEAKER_01]: You do not have that noble vision, Magandhya, by means of which you might know of and see nibbāna.

259
00:28:28,472 --> 00:28:30,594
[SPEAKER_01]: I have confidence in Master Gautama thus.

260
00:28:31,154 --> 00:28:37,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Master Gautama is capable of teaching me the dhamma in such a way that I can come to know health and to see nibbāna.

261
00:28:40,541 --> 00:28:45,284
[SPEAKER_01]: In Uganda, suppose there was a man born blind who could not see dark and light forms.

262
00:28:46,084 --> 00:28:50,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Then his friends and companions, his kinsmen and relatives, would bring a physician to treat him.

263
00:28:51,625 --> 00:28:54,007
[SPEAKER_01]: The physician would make a medicine for him.

264
00:28:54,748 --> 00:28:59,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, by means of that medicine, the man's vision would not arise or be purified.

265
00:29:00,232 --> 00:29:01,373
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, Magandya?

266
00:29:01,994 --> 00:29:04,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Would the doctor reap weariness and disappointment?

267
00:29:05,316 --> 00:29:06,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, Master Gautama.

268
00:29:07,158 --> 00:29:12,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So too, Magandya, if I were to teach you the dhamma thus, this is that help, this is that nibbāna.

269
00:29:13,323 --> 00:29:15,705
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not know that help or see nibbāna.

270
00:29:16,166 --> 00:29:18,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would be worrisome and troublesome for me.

271
00:29:22,202 --> 00:29:24,046
[SPEAKER_01]: I have confidence in Master Gautama.

272
00:29:24,347 --> 00:29:30,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Master Gautama is capable of teaching me the dhamma in such a way that I can come to know health and seem reborn.

273
00:29:36,347 --> 00:29:40,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Magandya, suppose there was a man born blind who could not see dark and light forms.

274
00:29:41,950 --> 00:29:49,053
[SPEAKER_01]: He might hear a man with good eyes saying, Good indeed, sirs, is a white cloth, beautiful, spotless and clean.

275
00:29:49,073 --> 00:29:50,774
[SPEAKER_01]: And he would go in search of a white cloth.

276
00:29:51,515 --> 00:29:54,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Then a man would cheat him with a dirty soiled garment.

277
00:29:55,076 --> 00:29:59,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, a good man hears a white cloth, and he would accept it and put it up.

278
00:30:00,239 --> 00:30:03,821
[SPEAKER_01]: And his friends and companions would bring a physician to treat him.

279
00:30:04,121 --> 00:30:05,542
[SPEAKER_01]: The physician would make medicine,

280
00:30:06,735 --> 00:30:13,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Ointments and counter-ointments, nasal treatments, and by means of that medicine, the man's vision would arise and be purified.

281
00:30:14,578 --> 00:30:21,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Together with the arising of his vision, his desire and liking for that dirty soiled garment would be abandoned.

282
00:30:22,662 --> 00:30:30,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he might burn with indignation and ill will towards that man and might think that he ought to be killed thus.

283
00:30:31,251 --> 00:30:42,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Indeed, I've long been tricked and treated, and defrauded by this man's, these dirty soiled garments, when he told me, good man, here's a white cloth, blue, beautiful, spotless and clean.

284
00:30:45,636 --> 00:30:51,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So to Magandiya, if I were to teach you the dhamma, this, this is that health, this is that nibbāna.

285
00:30:52,158 --> 00:30:54,099
[SPEAKER_01]: You might know health and see nibbāna.

286
00:30:55,123 --> 00:31:02,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Together with the arising of your vision, your desire and lust for the five aggregates affected by assumption might be abandoned.

287
00:31:04,097 --> 00:31:09,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, perhaps, you might think, indeed, I have been long tricked and cheated and defrauded by this mind.

288
00:31:10,883 --> 00:31:16,468
[SPEAKER_01]: For when assumption, I have... when assuming, I have been assuming just to material form.

289
00:31:17,129 --> 00:31:18,870
[SPEAKER_01]: I have been assuming just to feeling.

290
00:31:19,351 --> 00:31:21,212
[SPEAKER_01]: I have been assuming just to perception.

291
00:31:21,313 --> 00:31:23,374
[SPEAKER_01]: I have been assuming just to determinations.

292
00:31:23,995 --> 00:31:26,657
[SPEAKER_01]: I have been assuming just to consciousness.

293
00:31:27,498 --> 00:31:31,719
[SPEAKER_01]: With my assumption as condition, being comes to be.

294
00:31:32,399 --> 00:31:33,879
[SPEAKER_01]: With being as condition, birth.

295
00:31:34,479 --> 00:31:40,600
[SPEAKER_01]: With birth as condition, aging and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair come to be.

296
00:31:41,320 --> 00:31:44,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Such is the origin of this wholeness of suffering.

297
00:31:47,101 --> 00:31:49,102
[SPEAKER_01]: I have confidence in Master Gautama thus.

298
00:31:49,122 --> 00:31:56,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Master Gautama is capable of teaching me the dhamma in such a way that I might rise up from the seat cured of my blindness.

299
00:31:59,313 --> 00:32:02,076
[SPEAKER_01]: associate with true men.

300
00:32:03,017 --> 00:32:05,679
[SPEAKER_01]: When you associate with true men, you will hear the true dhamma.

301
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:09,943
[SPEAKER_01]: When you hear the true dhamma, you will practice in accordance with the true dhamma.

302
00:32:10,764 --> 00:32:15,248
[SPEAKER_01]: When you practice in accordance with the true dhamma, you will know and see for yourself thus.

303
00:32:16,308 --> 00:32:18,790
[SPEAKER_01]: These are diseases, tumours and darts.

304
00:32:19,491 --> 00:32:23,174
[SPEAKER_01]: But here, these diseases, tumours and darts cease without remainder.

305
00:32:23,994 --> 00:32:28,158
[SPEAKER_01]: With the cessation of my assumption comes cessation of being.

306
00:32:29,019 --> 00:32:31,361
[SPEAKER_01]: With the cessation of being, cessation of birth.

307
00:32:31,961 --> 00:32:37,866
[SPEAKER_01]: With the cessation of birth, ageing and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair cease.

308
00:32:38,747 --> 00:32:42,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Such is the cessation with the whole, this whole mess of suffering.

309
00:32:44,976 --> 00:32:50,138
[SPEAKER_01]: When this was said, the Wanderer Magandhiya said, Magnificent Master Gautama, magnificent!

310
00:32:50,978 --> 00:33:07,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Master Gautama has made the dhamma clear in many ways, as though he were turning upright, or had been overthrown, revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost, or holding up a lamp in the dark for those with eyesight to see forms.

311
00:33:09,004 --> 00:33:13,706
[SPEAKER_01]: I go to Master Gautama for refuge of the dhamma, and to the Saṅgha of Bhikkhus.

312
00:33:14,716 --> 00:33:17,337
[SPEAKER_01]: I would receive the going forth under the Master Gautama.

313
00:33:18,297 --> 00:33:20,097
[SPEAKER_01]: I would receive the full admission.

314
00:33:21,997 --> 00:33:33,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Magandiya, one who formerly belonged to the other sect and desires the going forth on full admission in this dhamma and discipline, lives on probation for four months.

315
00:33:33,440 --> 00:33:40,761
[SPEAKER_01]: At the end of the four months, if the bhikkhus are satisfied with him, they give him the going forth on full admission to the bhikkhu state.

316
00:33:42,148 --> 00:33:44,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But I recognize individual differences in this matter.

317
00:33:47,115 --> 00:33:55,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable Sir, if those who formerly belonged to another sect and desire the going forth and full mission in its dhamma and discipline, live on probation for four months,

318
00:33:57,253 --> 00:34:07,517
[SPEAKER_01]: And, if at the end of the four months the bhikkhu is being satisfied with them, give them the going forth and full admission to the bhikkhu state, then I will live on probation for four years.

319
00:34:07,557 --> 00:34:14,780
[SPEAKER_01]: At the end of four years, if the bhikkhus are satisfied with me, let them give me the going forth and full admission to the bhikkhu state.

320
00:34:16,441 --> 00:34:22,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the wanderer Magandiya received the going forth under the Blessed One, and he received the full admission.

321
00:34:23,517 --> 00:34:45,880
[SPEAKER_01]: And soon, not long after his full admission, dwelling alone, withdrawn, diligent, ardent and resolute, the Venerable Magandīr, by realizing for himself, with direct knowledge, here and now, entered upon and abided in that supreme goal of the holy life, for the sake of which clansmen rightly go forth, from the home life into homelessness.

322
00:34:47,067 --> 00:34:53,933
[SPEAKER_01]: He directly knew, birth is destroyed, the holy light has been lived, what had to be done has been done.

323
00:34:54,573 --> 00:34:56,915
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no more coming to any state of being.

324
00:34:57,556 --> 00:35:00,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Venerable Magandya became one of the elements.

325
00:35:12,728 --> 00:35:14,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I like the fact that he wasn't

326
00:35:15,467 --> 00:35:16,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, he was determined.

327
00:35:17,549 --> 00:35:21,772
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't really matter if he went forth, if he was still going to do it.

328
00:35:21,792 --> 00:35:22,613
[SPEAKER_01]: He listened to it.

329
00:35:22,753 --> 00:35:23,193
[SPEAKER_01]: He listened to it.

330
00:35:23,213 --> 00:35:27,436
[SPEAKER_01]: He'd take on the Buddhist teaching and go for it, no matter how long it took.

331
00:35:27,937 --> 00:35:29,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he doesn't want to.

332
00:35:30,599 --> 00:35:38,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Going forth is just an external means, a statement, a livelihood and so on.

333
00:35:38,405 --> 00:35:42,728
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, for somebody who's been feeling this through the dhamma back, right there and then,

334
00:35:43,418 --> 00:35:44,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it doesn't matter.

335
00:35:45,159 --> 00:35:48,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Four months, four years, doesn't matter, because you'll be doing the same thing.

336
00:35:49,021 --> 00:35:54,464
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be practicing, withdraw, renunciation, dwelling resolute.

337
00:35:55,625 --> 00:35:56,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, resolute.

338
00:35:56,826 --> 00:35:57,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

339
00:35:58,087 --> 00:36:00,208
[SPEAKER_00]: They're basically dwelling resolute.

340
00:36:02,409 --> 00:36:12,295
[SPEAKER_00]: One thing is to understand, but another thing is to stick to it, for as long as it is necessary, for all of the assumptions and habits of desires, fame.

341
00:36:13,467 --> 00:36:14,848
[SPEAKER_00]: For some people, it will take longer.

342
00:36:14,888 --> 00:36:17,590
[SPEAKER_00]: For some people, it will take shorter time.

343
00:36:18,851 --> 00:36:20,092
[SPEAKER_01]: But without being resolute.

344
00:36:20,532 --> 00:36:21,813
[SPEAKER_00]: But exactly, yeah.

345
00:36:21,853 --> 00:36:23,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Resolution needs to be there.

346
00:36:23,574 --> 00:36:27,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, resolution is possible only once understanding has arisen.

347
00:36:28,998 --> 00:36:35,542
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know what you're supposed to be resolute about, well, then all the resolution in the world won't help you.

348
00:36:36,543 --> 00:36:40,186
[SPEAKER_01]: But what about restraint, if you say, never again?

349
00:36:41,086 --> 00:36:41,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

350
00:36:43,206 --> 00:36:56,257
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, again, never again might free, if they stick to it, might free people from sensual desire.

351
00:36:56,677 --> 00:37:04,524
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still not necessarily... doesn't mean that they will have the right view in terms of dhamma and nibbāna.

352
00:37:05,589 --> 00:37:10,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the Buddha did say, even the two-year-old can experience this passion and this thirst in regard to sensuality.

353
00:37:11,733 --> 00:37:13,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And free himself from it.

354
00:37:15,135 --> 00:37:20,498
[SPEAKER_00]: But he can't free himself from other assumptions, assumptions of self, of conceit, and so on.

355
00:37:20,698 --> 00:37:27,282
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, it would be much easier for somebody who's free from sensuality to then understand the dhamma, than from somebody who isn't.

356
00:37:28,479 --> 00:37:28,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

357
00:37:28,699 --> 00:37:31,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he already kind of understood the principle of assumption.

358
00:37:32,621 --> 00:37:41,425
[SPEAKER_00]: It just needs to be pointed out, that very fact, that padāna, of sensual pleasures, of craving, has been obviously abandoned.

359
00:37:42,286 --> 00:37:50,329
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he needs to dwell on the nature of that abandonment, and then apply the same to the sense of self, and his conceit, and then he'll be in nibbāna.

360
00:37:51,010 --> 00:37:55,512
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, he can't do that unless he's... somebody points that out to him, somebody who knows.

361
00:38:07,592 --> 00:38:17,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, to me also, resolute means you don't take... you see the goal, you take nothing less than that.

362
00:38:17,198 --> 00:38:18,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

363
00:38:18,159 --> 00:38:19,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you're striving for.

364
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:23,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, resolution can be basically... can take many forms.

365
00:38:23,622 --> 00:38:25,944
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be resolute for the rest of your life.

366
00:38:27,115 --> 00:38:39,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Any, any, any sensual pleasure that comes within the range of your senses, you will basically override with a vision of pain, and dirt, and burning embers.

367
00:38:40,059 --> 00:38:40,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

368
00:38:40,719 --> 00:38:42,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how they actually are.

369
00:38:43,580 --> 00:38:48,461
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not necessarily see that, but you took it of faith, because the Buddha said it.

370
00:38:49,561 --> 00:38:55,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you keep applying it, because it is so, it will take its roots, and then you will see itself.

371
00:38:56,539 --> 00:39:04,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you can take on faith wrong instruction and then no amount of applying it will result in seeing it clearly as such, because it's not as such.

372
00:39:04,886 --> 00:39:06,648
[SPEAKER_00]: But sensuality is as such.

373
00:39:07,288 --> 00:39:17,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you kind of emulate in seeing it as such, like overriding with your resolution, will result in eventually seeing it for what it is, as such.

374
00:39:19,579 --> 00:39:21,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's basically those people who practice on faith.

375
00:39:22,301 --> 00:39:24,443
[SPEAKER_00]: They take on faith and they just stick to it.

376
00:39:26,450 --> 00:39:28,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it's been made pretty clear.

377
00:39:30,932 --> 00:39:41,175
[SPEAKER_00]: And I don't think, like, you know, if there is a resolution, if there is a restraint, if there is a virtue established, I don't think it will take too long for one to start seeing that actually they are painful.

378
00:39:42,712 --> 00:39:53,635
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't be before too long before it becomes apparent that the pain of restraint is not of restraint, but it's revealed by restraint and it's of the senses, of the desire.

379
00:39:53,695 --> 00:39:56,156
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the first point of contact.

380
00:39:56,176 --> 00:39:56,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Make sense?

381
00:39:57,557 --> 00:40:01,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Something like with the essential pleasure, first point of contact is pain.

382
00:40:04,939 --> 00:40:05,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

383
00:40:06,672 --> 00:40:12,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else, all the pleasure you then get from giving in to those desires, is built on top of those burning embers.

384
00:40:14,398 --> 00:40:17,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means they themselves are certainly not pleasant.

385
00:40:20,102 --> 00:40:22,604
[SPEAKER_01]: The origin of sensual pleasures?

386
00:40:22,624 --> 00:40:23,945
[SPEAKER_01]: The origin of their arising?

387
00:40:24,185 --> 00:40:33,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the arising of sensual pleasure, basically, would... you can pin down on your assumption of... in regard to sensual pleasure, your assumption that they were unpleasant.

388
00:40:35,201 --> 00:40:36,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you will crave for them.

389
00:40:39,945 --> 00:40:45,370
[SPEAKER_00]: And craving for them will then make them, make you regard them as even more pleasant, and so on, feed itself.

390
00:40:47,011 --> 00:40:50,454
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop craving for them, sensuality cannot arise.

391
00:40:51,655 --> 00:40:58,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Agreeable, beautiful objects in the world will still arise, but they, they will not induce sensual desire.

392
00:41:01,965 --> 00:41:11,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that sensuality, like beautiful sights, for example, are confined to the domain of sight, to the eyes.

393
00:41:14,047 --> 00:41:17,828
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is a sensual desire to see something beautiful.

394
00:41:19,589 --> 00:41:21,949
[SPEAKER_00]: If you say no to it, you will experience pain.

395
00:41:24,230 --> 00:41:26,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the desire is, pain.

396
00:41:28,290 --> 00:41:34,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And people don't experience pain, because their minds are already far out in the anticipation of satisfaction and desire.

397
00:41:35,693 --> 00:41:39,375
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're already running, even before the dog started yanking them.

398
00:41:42,716 --> 00:41:45,758
[SPEAKER_00]: But all you have to do is just stop, and the pain will arise.

399
00:41:46,978 --> 00:41:53,221
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, as I said, with some instruction, and diligence, and resolve it, but if you become a parent,

400
00:41:54,767 --> 00:41:58,228
[SPEAKER_00]: That pain, no matter like big or small sometimes, doesn't matter.

401
00:41:58,769 --> 00:42:01,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the pain of the actual sensual touch.

402
00:42:02,090 --> 00:42:08,033
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the pain of craving present simultaneously with an agreeable beautiful object in the world.

403
00:42:09,093 --> 00:42:10,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, you remove the craving.

404
00:42:11,594 --> 00:42:16,256
[SPEAKER_00]: And what you have is just beautiful objects in the world, but no desire for them.

405
00:42:25,382 --> 00:42:33,405
[SPEAKER_00]: And that brings you to the, well, other approach, the approach of Four Noble Truths.

406
00:42:34,466 --> 00:42:36,987
[SPEAKER_00]: With dhamma, with craving, suffering is.

407
00:42:37,667 --> 00:42:39,388
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever there is pain, there is craving.

408
00:42:42,409 --> 00:42:45,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you suffer on account of sensuality, it's because you crave.

409
00:42:46,010 --> 00:42:47,191
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what sensuality is.

410
00:42:48,162 --> 00:42:50,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality is craving for sense-objects.

411
00:42:50,703 --> 00:42:57,986
[SPEAKER_00]: That, as a whole, as a kind of, as a unit, that's what sensuality is.

412
00:42:58,066 --> 00:43:02,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for sense-objects, or pleasures derived through the sense-objects.

413
00:43:05,109 --> 00:43:05,969
[SPEAKER_00]: That is sensuality.

414
00:43:06,810 --> 00:43:09,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you can rightly say, sensuality is painful and unpleasant.

415
00:43:10,251 --> 00:43:15,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't have sensuality, then, without the main component of craving for the sense-objects.

416
00:43:20,860 --> 00:43:24,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's seen a danger of craving.

417
00:43:26,162 --> 00:43:27,082
[SPEAKER_01]: We see the danger.

418
00:43:27,783 --> 00:43:29,063
[SPEAKER_01]: There is pain, there is suffering.

419
00:43:29,103 --> 00:43:36,646
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger of craving, basically, like... Craving for pleasure derived from sense objects.

420
00:43:37,526 --> 00:43:44,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sense objects cannot be discerned if there are no operating, functional, living, healthy senses.

421
00:43:45,683 --> 00:43:47,364
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's something that's completely obscured.

422
00:43:47,985 --> 00:43:51,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, the mind is already out there with the actual pleasures that you derive from it.

423
00:43:51,908 --> 00:43:55,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you took everything that precedes those pleasures, you took that for granted.

424
00:43:56,972 --> 00:43:57,793
[SPEAKER_00]: You made it be.

425
00:43:59,174 --> 00:43:59,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhāva is there.

426
00:44:00,355 --> 00:44:01,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual being is there.

427
00:44:02,376 --> 00:44:04,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind is far.

428
00:44:09,150 --> 00:44:27,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, as I said, for example, if your mind is fully dependent on the pleasures derived by the sense base and sense objects, if something happens to sense bases and sense objects, and something will happen because they're not in your control, then the mind will be cut away with it.

429
00:44:27,741 --> 00:44:28,842
[SPEAKER_00]: That is what death is.

430
00:44:30,449 --> 00:44:34,630
[SPEAKER_00]: You're being swept with the floods that sweep away your body, your five senses.

431
00:44:35,850 --> 00:44:43,372
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you climb down that tree, those sense trees, on the safe ground, when they're taken down, you will crash with them.

432
00:44:47,773 --> 00:44:48,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is the danger.

433
00:44:48,613 --> 00:44:49,413
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a real danger.

434
00:44:49,433 --> 00:44:50,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what death is.

435
00:44:51,074 --> 00:44:56,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Death cannot touch you, if your mind is withdrawn from the dependence on the pleasure of the senses.

436
00:45:04,222 --> 00:45:11,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody just... all you have to do is just contemplate losing your sight, losing your ears, losing your life.

437
00:45:13,104 --> 00:45:16,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, like, say there is an imminent danger of death.

438
00:45:17,545 --> 00:45:22,567
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of sense desire will override that, no matter how strong it was before.

439
00:45:22,667 --> 00:45:30,669
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that the very next second you might be dead, you will drop everything you were doing, everything you want to do, everything you were engaged with.

440
00:45:33,321 --> 00:45:35,882
[SPEAKER_00]: And most likely, a normal person would freak out then.

441
00:45:36,683 --> 00:45:37,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Become religious.

442
00:45:38,024 --> 00:45:39,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Or become religious, yeah.

443
00:45:39,724 --> 00:45:44,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Start praying to the Divine Providence and develop a faith that everything will end up well for you.

444
00:45:45,828 --> 00:45:47,649
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically the whole purpose of that.

445
00:45:47,809 --> 00:45:48,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it's the same.

446
00:45:49,009 --> 00:45:51,231
[SPEAKER_00]: It emulates the principle of sensuality.

447
00:45:52,251 --> 00:45:55,613
[SPEAKER_00]: You were hit by the pain, you cover it up with a pleasant desire.

448
00:45:57,007 --> 00:46:01,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You were hit by the pain of non-sensual nature.

449
00:46:01,830 --> 00:46:04,272
[SPEAKER_00]: You cover it up with the assumptions of everything will be fine.

450
00:46:04,492 --> 00:46:06,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, pleasure, peaceful, it's okay.

451
00:46:08,734 --> 00:46:12,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole purpose of human religiousness and superstition is a cover-up.

452
00:46:13,137 --> 00:46:14,798
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just extension of sensuality.

453
00:46:19,881 --> 00:46:22,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, it's another type of upādāna, as the Buddha said.

454
00:46:24,784 --> 00:46:25,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla Bhātraparamāsa.

455
00:46:30,130 --> 00:46:46,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, belief, assuming, holding on to virtues, duties, extension into religion and so on, holding on to views, assuming the views, that's also comes on brain.

456
00:46:58,352 --> 00:46:59,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a bit to know what, you know,

457
00:47:01,025 --> 00:47:12,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the greatest of happiness in most people is a fully functional, vastly strong body that can take you around and get all those pleasures.

458
00:47:12,827 --> 00:47:17,548
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the peak of happiness that you can imagine.

459
00:47:17,648 --> 00:47:20,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is simultaneously the peak of the danger.

460
00:47:23,349 --> 00:47:29,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Means like that's when your dependence, your blind dependence, like you're not aware of it.

461
00:47:30,140 --> 00:47:36,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're too far out with the expectations of pleasures, that's with the dependence in its full highest swing.

462
00:47:41,869 --> 00:47:45,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Because a person might start withdrawing from the central pleasures when senses start failing.

463
00:47:46,708 --> 00:47:53,152
[SPEAKER_00]: However, if you wait for your senses to start failing, and then withdraw, well, you are not withdrawing, you are being withdrawn.

464
00:47:53,192 --> 00:48:00,697
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what you're going to have, then you're going to have views, and all sorts of assumptions based on that, that now you cannot undo.

465
00:48:00,717 --> 00:48:03,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's kind of too late, basically.

466
00:48:03,779 --> 00:48:05,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Your dependence on it has been fortified.

467
00:48:10,083 --> 00:48:11,244
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think you're at old age,

468
00:48:11,852 --> 00:48:13,773
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the danger of old age, basically.

469
00:48:13,973 --> 00:48:19,636
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, theoretically, it's still possible to uproot it all and become an arahant, but highly unlikely.

470
00:48:20,897 --> 00:48:21,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

471
00:48:21,157 --> 00:48:29,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, I mean, the habit of dependence on the senses has been cultivated many fold, much longer than for somebody who was 20 or 30 years old.

472
00:48:30,823 --> 00:48:39,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means all sorts of views and assumptions on top of that have arisen, and now the senses have, you know, declined.

473
00:48:40,217 --> 00:48:50,123
[SPEAKER_00]: And sure, your ability to kind of experience the central pleasure has declined, but not the assumption of the pleasures of the senses and so on.

474
00:48:51,865 --> 00:48:52,505
[SPEAKER_01]: That has stayed.

475
00:48:53,386 --> 00:48:57,668
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you have no kind of reference point for that to undo it.

476
00:48:59,149 --> 00:49:09,396
[SPEAKER_00]: When your senses were still there, you could see a direct link, direct dependence on your assumptions upon the central pleasures and everything else, and the actual operating senses.

477
00:49:10,425 --> 00:49:19,551
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, the senses are gone, and you're so oblivious in regard to them, ignorant, deluded, indolent, it's virtually impossible.

478
00:49:21,392 --> 00:49:23,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Theoretically, it's possible, but highly unlikely.

479
00:49:25,534 --> 00:49:27,276
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the longer you wait...

480
00:49:27,336 --> 00:49:29,157
[SPEAKER_00]: I would have said that, actually, in that sutta, you just read it.

481
00:49:29,697 --> 00:49:33,079
[SPEAKER_00]: The longer you engage with sensuality, the stronger it gets.

482
00:49:35,981 --> 00:49:37,482
[SPEAKER_00]: The more dependent upon you become.

483
00:49:38,846 --> 00:49:41,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Means the more painful it will be, saying no to it.

484
00:49:45,108 --> 00:49:49,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, the greater pain will be revealed by saying no to it.

485
00:49:49,529 --> 00:49:50,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And mind cannot take the pain.

486
00:49:51,090 --> 00:49:52,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Untrained mind cannot take the pain.

487
00:49:53,290 --> 00:49:56,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And what will untrained mind, which cannot take the pain, do?

488
00:49:56,912 --> 00:49:59,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Try to avoid the pain, but it only means that you know it.

489
00:49:59,692 --> 00:50:02,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Means of survival.

490
00:50:02,393 --> 00:50:05,014
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up revolving around that which causes you pain.

491
00:50:05,914 --> 00:50:07,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You're using that to avoid that pain.

492
00:50:09,975 --> 00:50:12,997
[SPEAKER_01]: But what about you can wean yourself off of sensual pleasures?

493
00:50:13,978 --> 00:50:15,118
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you can't do that either.

494
00:50:15,218 --> 00:50:16,159
[SPEAKER_01]: But just a little bit.

495
00:50:16,559 --> 00:50:16,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

496
00:50:17,019 --> 00:50:17,680
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to have a lot.

497
00:50:17,720 --> 00:50:35,130
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said, in regard to the being, and there is a being of sensual pleasures as well, he said basically that as much as a large lump of excrements stinks, even the small bits of excrements share the same stink.

498
00:50:36,062 --> 00:50:37,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Same foul smell.

499
00:50:37,904 --> 00:50:39,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for the being of any kind.

500
00:50:40,066 --> 00:50:41,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for sensuality.

501
00:50:41,687 --> 00:50:48,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, weaning yourself of excrement, with little excrement, is not weaning yourself of excrement smell at all.

502
00:50:48,534 --> 00:50:49,995
[SPEAKER_00]: The excrement smell is still present.

503
00:50:51,096 --> 00:50:52,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter the amount of it.

504
00:50:54,639 --> 00:50:55,620
[SPEAKER_00]: How big the lung is.

505
00:51:11,542 --> 00:51:21,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, that someone is doing something innocent enough, you know, not so bad, it's still a distraction, like watching movies.

506
00:51:21,851 --> 00:51:23,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, watching movies, distracting yourself.

507
00:51:23,953 --> 00:51:26,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, it's not a horrible thing.

508
00:51:28,417 --> 00:51:30,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You're watching action movies or whatever.

509
00:51:31,132 --> 00:51:37,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the person sees, okay, well, that's distracting, that's got nothing to do with dhamma, it's got nothing to do with holy love.

510
00:51:37,675 --> 00:51:44,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, I'll watch something with... it's got a... you know, connected to dhamma.

511
00:51:44,438 --> 00:51:45,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Dhamma movie.

512
00:51:45,739 --> 00:51:46,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Eh, well, there is no such a thing.

513
00:51:47,320 --> 00:51:51,182
[SPEAKER_01]: But then I... well, connected to old age and death.

514
00:51:51,202 --> 00:51:53,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, I'm weaning myself of...

515
00:51:54,892 --> 00:52:05,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but see, the point is, you turn a blind eye to the fact that the purpose of a movie is... sorry, the purpose of the movies, in general, is entertainment.

516
00:52:06,779 --> 00:52:07,619
[SPEAKER_00]: It's distraction.

517
00:52:07,759 --> 00:52:09,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's taking your mind away.

518
00:52:10,461 --> 00:52:15,744
[SPEAKER_00]: And that taking your mind away, it's nothing other than moha, the delusion, the indolence of the mind.

519
00:52:17,035 --> 00:52:25,664
[SPEAKER_00]: As I was saying yesterday, delusion is... it's not... it's not a kind of a hidden secret truth that you are not aware of.

520
00:52:25,865 --> 00:52:26,585
[SPEAKER_00]: You are deluded.

521
00:52:26,626 --> 00:52:26,826
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

522
00:52:26,846 --> 00:52:32,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Delusion is an attitude of mind, in the same sense, anger is and lust is.

523
00:52:32,952 --> 00:52:35,135
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's lust aversion and delusion.

524
00:52:36,796 --> 00:52:47,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sure, you might watch a dhamma movie that does not have the explicit sensuality, provocativeness, and so on, but the fact is that you're engaging in the indolence of mind.

525
00:52:48,382 --> 00:52:48,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

526
00:52:48,843 --> 00:52:50,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You let your mind go where the movie is.

527
00:52:51,184 --> 00:52:52,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Let the story unfold.

528
00:52:53,145 --> 00:52:58,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Take your mind on a ride, which is not necessarily sensual ride, but it's the ride of the vision.

529
00:52:58,868 --> 00:53:04,992
[SPEAKER_00]: The ride of indolence, which is basically that which is kind of undermining, sorry, underlying both

530
00:53:05,912 --> 00:53:06,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust and anger.

531
00:53:07,153 --> 00:53:10,174
[SPEAKER_00]: If your mind is never indolent, it cannot give in to lust nor anger.

532
00:53:11,295 --> 00:53:17,358
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are practicing delusion, basically, if you're practicing indolence, means you remain liable to lust.

533
00:53:17,558 --> 00:53:19,199
[SPEAKER_00]: You remain accessible to lust.

534
00:53:19,819 --> 00:53:20,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Lust remains possible.

535
00:53:21,640 --> 00:53:27,623
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sure, you can watch all sorts of documentaries and informative movies and whatever else.

536
00:53:27,983 --> 00:53:35,227
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is, your mind goes in a state of indolence, which means when the lust comes, you will not be fortified in regard to it.

537
00:53:38,677 --> 00:53:40,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no excuse for this.

538
00:53:41,059 --> 00:53:43,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, non-indolence is just...

539
00:53:44,261 --> 00:53:52,506
[SPEAKER_00]: If you remain non-indolent, you will not give in to any distraction, no matter how informative the inverted comma is.

540
00:53:52,726 --> 00:53:59,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Because dhamma is to be known individually, internally, by the wise, not on a big screen.

541
00:54:01,312 --> 00:54:05,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if it's the movie about the Buddha's life, again, it could be watched as distraction.

542
00:54:06,784 --> 00:54:10,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only way to remain mindful of a movie is to basically not give in to the movie.

543
00:54:11,047 --> 00:54:12,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And that means, why watch it in the first place?

544
00:54:16,150 --> 00:54:18,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Same like reading novels or books of any kind.

545
00:54:20,113 --> 00:54:21,494
[SPEAKER_00]: They're meant to take the mind away.

546
00:54:22,474 --> 00:54:28,158
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not as bad as, you know, full-on pursuit of sensuality, you know, in a binging manner.

547
00:54:28,838 --> 00:54:35,623
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's certainly maintaining the indolence of the mind, maintaining the delusion, maintaining the ignoring.

548
00:54:36,870 --> 00:54:55,801
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's an important point, because many people still have notions and assumptions that delusion and even avijja, it's kind of, you know, this metaphysical ignorance that is then revealed by some extraordinary transcendental experience.

549
00:54:55,861 --> 00:54:57,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

550
00:54:57,762 --> 00:55:00,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something you engage most often.

551
00:55:01,545 --> 00:55:03,786
[SPEAKER_00]: More often than sensuality, more often than anger.

552
00:55:06,032 --> 00:55:07,032
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what avijjā is.

553
00:55:07,252 --> 00:55:08,313
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what delusion is.

554
00:55:08,933 --> 00:55:11,093
[SPEAKER_00]: It's indolence of the mind.

555
00:55:11,413 --> 00:55:12,474
[SPEAKER_00]: It's muddleness.

556
00:55:13,534 --> 00:55:14,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Lack of clarity.

557
00:55:15,214 --> 00:55:16,234
[SPEAKER_00]: And you maintain that.

558
00:55:16,334 --> 00:55:17,195
[SPEAKER_00]: People maintain that.

559
00:55:17,855 --> 00:55:19,175
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain it through sensuality.

560
00:55:19,215 --> 00:55:20,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain it through anger.

561
00:55:20,896 --> 00:55:22,436
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain it through ignorant choices.

562
00:55:22,836 --> 00:55:25,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain it through distraction and dependence on distraction.

563
00:55:25,977 --> 00:55:33,999
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you remove a distraction from... Well, remove all of the distractions from a normal person out there in the world.

564
00:55:34,019 --> 00:55:35,279
[SPEAKER_00]: And they'll freak out.

565
00:55:36,171 --> 00:55:41,314
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the boredom will start creeping in, and then the boredom will reveal a terror behind it.

566
00:55:43,976 --> 00:55:52,321
[SPEAKER_00]: A real genuine fear of, and a threat, a real genuine threat of sinking into your own mind, and drowning there, where man lives.

567
00:55:53,082 --> 00:55:56,444
[SPEAKER_00]: So, quickly, provide with some distractions.

568
00:55:56,584 --> 00:55:57,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Give me something to do.

569
00:56:00,370 --> 00:56:03,473
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it's devoted to waitfulness, it's saying.

570
00:56:03,513 --> 00:56:03,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

571
00:56:04,574 --> 00:56:09,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, it sounds nice, like, you know, it's a, it's quite a heavy thing to do.

572
00:56:09,640 --> 00:56:16,827
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a heavy thing to do initially, but until you, until you still have the pull of the senses and so on, it feels like a heavy work.

573
00:56:16,947 --> 00:56:18,990
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is, it is a heavy work initially.

574
00:56:19,390 --> 00:56:21,793
[SPEAKER_00]: As we spoke in, was a couple of days ago,

575
00:56:22,725 --> 00:56:25,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a herdsman looking after the cows.

576
00:56:25,826 --> 00:56:33,610
[SPEAKER_00]: While the cows are still wild and unrestrained, it's going to be a lot of work to keep them away from the paddy field, from eating other people's crop.

577
00:56:35,030 --> 00:56:38,232
[SPEAKER_00]: But once they have tamed and calmed down, then it's just pure pleasure.

578
00:56:38,252 --> 00:56:38,812
[SPEAKER_00]: You just sit.

579
00:56:38,832 --> 00:56:39,812
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even have to move.

580
00:56:40,172 --> 00:56:40,793
[SPEAKER_01]: They listen to you.

581
00:56:40,893 --> 00:56:41,413
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't go.

582
00:56:44,154 --> 00:56:51,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, initially, for a person who never practiced wakefulness, it's going to be the hardest task in the world.

583
00:56:52,055 --> 00:56:54,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's devoted to awakening.

584
00:56:54,276 --> 00:56:56,077
[SPEAKER_01]: This means less sleeping.

585
00:56:56,217 --> 00:56:58,958
[SPEAKER_00]: It means less sleep even when you're awake.

586
00:56:59,178 --> 00:56:59,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

587
00:57:00,118 --> 00:57:01,639
[SPEAKER_00]: It means less distractions.

588
00:57:01,779 --> 00:57:04,320
[SPEAKER_00]: It means not resting mentally.

589
00:57:04,841 --> 00:57:06,861
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean you're fully engaged in constant effort.

590
00:57:07,422 --> 00:57:09,843
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you're engaged in non-resting.

591
00:57:11,699 --> 00:57:20,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the simile of a cow, that if it sits down, it's being eaten by the animals, devoured by the animals that live on the ground.

592
00:57:20,804 --> 00:57:27,528
[SPEAKER_00]: If it leans on something, stands up and leans on something, it's devoured by the animals that live on the things, on the trees, it leans and so on.

593
00:57:27,568 --> 00:57:34,212
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, no position of the mind, you give in to that kind of, ah, just to rest for five minutes.

594
00:57:34,232 --> 00:57:35,652
[SPEAKER_00]: You can rest your body all you want.

595
00:57:35,872 --> 00:57:36,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And shut it.

596
00:57:36,453 --> 00:57:38,514
[SPEAKER_00]: But mind, exactly, no shutting up.

597
00:57:39,820 --> 00:57:40,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

598
00:57:40,100 --> 00:57:44,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Because actually, shutting off is not necessary.

599
00:57:44,641 --> 00:57:46,101
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why delusion is a habit.

600
00:57:46,701 --> 00:57:49,822
[SPEAKER_00]: A vijja is a habit that's being maintained through a vijja.

601
00:57:51,422 --> 00:57:55,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance is determined by ignorance.

602
00:57:55,323 --> 00:57:57,243
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said that.

603
00:57:57,263 --> 00:57:58,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And Nibbāna mentions it as well.

604
00:58:01,164 --> 00:58:06,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So the point is, once the mind is trained in wakefulness, wakefulness is effortless.

605
00:58:10,007 --> 00:58:11,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it ceases to be a chore.

606
00:58:12,108 --> 00:58:13,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes a very pleasant, peaceful abiding.

607
00:58:13,929 --> 00:58:14,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes nibbāna.

608
00:58:26,875 --> 00:58:36,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, obviously, yeah, the pull to go back to Avijjā is the... Well, as long as Avijjā is not entirely uprooted, exactly, Avijjā will try to maintain itself.

609
00:58:38,927 --> 00:58:51,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, basically, that a person sees danger in sensuality, sees it as burning embers, so should the indolence of the mind be seen as well, non-wakefulness.

610
00:58:52,180 --> 00:58:56,604
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is, what I said, the ones who are not mindful are already as if dead.

611
00:59:24,273 --> 00:59:24,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

612
00:59:24,553 --> 00:59:25,894
[SPEAKER_01]: So, living is quite difficult.

613
00:59:27,595 --> 00:59:28,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Real living, in that case.

614
00:59:28,576 --> 00:59:30,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Real living is a full-time task.

615
00:59:30,957 --> 00:59:32,579
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's again what Nyanavila Therese said.

616
00:59:33,719 --> 00:59:38,222
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not some... Life is not something you just float and fly over and go through.

617
00:59:38,943 --> 00:59:41,605
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes actually full-time task.

618
00:59:42,885 --> 00:59:49,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Even for the one who is not necessarily practicing for Arahantship, just wants to live authentically, wants to live responsibly, wants to live mindfully.

619
00:59:52,472 --> 00:59:53,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is that anyway.

620
00:59:54,432 --> 01:00:03,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if for a patujana, a sutta patujana, uninstructed, without any regard, it's also... it's even worse, because he's making it worse through ignoring it.

621
01:00:04,478 --> 01:00:07,720
[SPEAKER_00]: He's making his itch worse through constantly scratching it.

622
01:00:08,560 --> 01:00:16,365
[SPEAKER_00]: He's making it powerful each time, which means each time he'll need to work even harder to undo it.

623
01:00:33,365 --> 01:00:38,689
[SPEAKER_00]: You can never have too much of mindfulness.

624
01:00:40,290 --> 01:00:45,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you have too much of anything else, mindfulness will even it out, if mindfulness is established.

625
01:00:53,979 --> 01:00:55,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity of the mind.

626
01:00:55,780 --> 01:00:58,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-muddledness, so to speak.

627
01:00:59,422 --> 01:01:00,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Clear thinking.

628
01:01:06,266 --> 01:01:09,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And restraint in the senses.

629
01:01:09,928 --> 01:01:19,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, even a very little of the instruction from the suttas and the buddha will be required, because the ground is very fertile, set up.

630
01:01:20,454 --> 01:01:24,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is there, ready, in the right order, purified.

631
01:01:24,597 --> 01:01:29,700
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you need very little instruction to fix it up, once and for all.

